1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,181
Suppose a customer asks for a refund thirty-five days after purchase.

2
00:00:04,181 --> 00:00:06,842
Yesterday that was outside the refund window.

3
00:00:06,842 --> 00:00:11,404
Today, they're eligible for forty-five days, subject to the handbook's
usage limit.

4
00:00:11,404 --> 00:00:15,585
How do you get a support assistant to change its answer?

5
00:00:15,585 --> 00:00:23,568
This is LLM Adaptation Comparison: a Python project that tests three
ways to update the knowledge of a small language model.

6
00:00:23,583 --> 00:00:26,038
Here's the design in the README.

7
00:00:26,038 --> 00:00:28,084
Retrieval supplies a handbook passage.

8
00:00:28,084 --> 00:00:30,130
Few-shot prompting supplies worked examples.

9
00:00:30,130 --> 00:00:35,040
LoRA updates a small set of weights using PyTorch and Hugging Face.

10
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:38,723
All three methods answer the same eleven policy questions.

11
00:00:38,750 --> 00:00:41,413
Here are the files behind that comparison.

12
00:00:41,413 --> 00:00:48,259
Data holds the handbooks, rag contains the retrievers, and experiments
contains the prompts, model backends, and evaluation code.

13
00:00:48,259 --> 00:00:54,725
Open run dot py and you can trace one question all the way to a scored
answer.

14
00:00:54,750 --> 00:00:57,074
Notice the order of these lines.

15
00:00:57,074 --> 00:01:01,722
The runner retrieves the passage, builds the prompt, and generates one
answer.

16
00:01:01,722 --> 00:01:05,596
Then it scores that same answer against both handbook versions.

17
00:01:05,596 --> 00:01:11,793
That's how the stale column measures yesterday's answer against today's
policy, without asking the model again.

18
00:01:11,833 --> 00:01:14,050
Now we’re in the project itself.

19
00:01:14,050 --> 00:01:22,177
The retriever fits a word index over handbook sections, transforms a
question into that same vocabulary, and ranks passages by cosine
similarity.

20
00:01:22,177 --> 00:01:27,349
The demo function takes the highest-ranked passage and passes its text
to the model.

21
00:01:27,349 --> 00:01:31,781
Notice that version one and version two each get their own index.

22
00:01:31,781 --> 00:01:36,214
Mixing those passages would defeat the purpose of testing a policy
change.

23
00:01:36,250 --> 00:01:40,305
Before using the model, let’s test the code behind those measurements.

24
00:01:40,305 --> 00:01:44,361
This is the repository’s pytest suite running in the integrated
terminal.

25
00:01:44,361 --> 00:01:51,365
One test changes the order of the saved answers; the comparison still
has to match them by question identifier.

26
00:01:51,365 --> 00:01:55,052
Another introduces a duplicate that would hide a missing question.

27
00:01:55,052 --> 00:01:56,895
That case must be rejected.

28
00:01:56,895 --> 00:02:00,213
Here the tests finish and the process exits successfully.

29
00:02:00,250 --> 00:02:03,122
This is the training path used on Colab.

30
00:02:03,122 --> 00:02:05,276
The model loads in four-bit precision.

31
00:02:05,276 --> 00:02:10,661
For the first run, the code adds LoRA weights to the query and value
projections.

32
00:02:10,661 --> 00:02:16,406
For the update, it loads the existing adapter as trainable and continues
on the revised examples.

33
00:02:16,406 --> 00:02:21,073
The paired evaluation then compares the two checkpoints against the same
new targets.

34
00:02:21,073 --> 00:02:23,586
We’ll inspect those saved GPU answers shortly.

35
00:02:23,667 --> 00:02:25,923
Here’s the refund question running locally.

36
00:02:25,923 --> 00:02:31,564
The command asks how many days after purchase a customer can request a
full refund.

37
00:02:31,564 --> 00:02:33,821
First, watch which section comes back.

38
00:02:33,821 --> 00:02:38,709
Version one retrieves the standard refund window, and its passage says
thirty days.

39
00:02:38,709 --> 00:02:40,590
The model answers thirty days.

40
00:02:40,590 --> 00:02:46,231
Now the program rebuilds the index from version two and asks exactly the
same question.

41
00:02:46,231 --> 00:02:51,496
This time the updated passage says forty-five days, and the answer
changes to forty-five.

42
00:02:51,496 --> 00:02:54,880
This is live inference with the local Llama model.

43
00:02:54,880 --> 00:03:00,145
The controlled comparison uses Qwen on a T four GPU; those are separate
runs.

44
00:03:00,167 --> 00:03:04,344
A model can’t use a policy that its retriever never finds.

45
00:03:04,344 --> 00:03:07,382
Here the ranking harness reruns the paraphrased questions.

46
00:03:07,382 --> 00:03:12,319
In the last two columns, top one means the right passage came first.

47
00:03:12,319 --> 00:03:16,117
Top three means it was somewhere in the first three.

48
00:03:16,117 --> 00:03:21,054
On version two, whole-word matching found seven of eleven within the top
three.

49
00:03:21,054 --> 00:03:22,953
Short character sequences found ten.

50
00:03:22,953 --> 00:03:25,991
Adding the section title didn’t change those counts.

51
00:03:25,991 --> 00:03:29,029
This measures access to evidence, not answer correctness.

52
00:03:29,083 --> 00:03:31,947
Now look at the saved two-factor authentication answer.

53
00:03:31,947 --> 00:03:34,095
The updated handbook requires it now.

54
00:03:34,095 --> 00:03:37,316
The model calls it voluntary now and required later.

55
00:03:37,316 --> 00:03:40,896
A keyword check saw the word required and awarded credit.

56
00:03:40,896 --> 00:03:43,401
Reading the whole sentence reverses that decision.

57
00:03:43,401 --> 00:03:48,770
That one correction takes the updated LoRA score from five out of eleven
to four.

58
00:03:48,770 --> 00:03:52,350
The original output and the review decision are both preserved.

59
00:03:52,417 --> 00:03:56,322
These rows pair each answer before and after continued training.

60
00:03:56,322 --> 00:03:59,837
LoRA learned the new password-reset interval and backup-retention
period.

61
00:03:59,837 --> 00:04:04,913
It lost the two-factor rule and an unchanged fact, the Pro Plan
capacity.

62
00:04:04,913 --> 00:04:09,209
So the total stayed flat, even though four individual outcomes changed.

63
00:04:09,209 --> 00:04:18,191
The two training stages each used thirteen examples and twenty epochs;
the update took about one hundred twenty-nine seconds on the T four.

64
00:04:18,250 --> 00:04:19,828
Here’s the final comparison.

65
00:04:19,828 --> 00:04:24,167
With updated knowledge, both retrieval methods got eleven of eleven
correct.

66
00:04:24,167 --> 00:04:25,745
Few-shot prompting got ten.

67
00:04:25,745 --> 00:04:27,717
LoRA got four after review.

68
00:04:27,717 --> 00:04:34,422
For this small handbook, replacing the retrieval source was the most
reliable way to keep answers current.

69
00:04:34,422 --> 00:04:39,550
Eleven synthetic facts and one matched training run don’t establish a
universal winner.

70
00:04:39,550 --> 00:04:45,073
The next useful test is a larger set of policies and repeated training
runs.

71
00:04:45,073 --> 00:04:49,806
The code, raw answers, and report are all available alongside these
results.
